[Cloudflare](https://www.cloudflare.com/) runs a global network that provides CDN, DNS, security, and developer services such as Workers. With Cloudflare OAuth, third-party apps can sign users in with their Cloudflare account and access Cloudflare resources on their behalf, without asking them to share long-lived API tokens.

# How does the integration work?

You can use the Cloudflare OAuth adapter in Appwrite Auth to let users sign in with their Cloudflare account. Tools that manage Workers, DNS records, or other Cloudflare resources can sign users in with the same account they're about to manage. The session stores Cloudflare's access token as `providerAccessToken`, so your server can call the Cloudflare API for the user.

Appwrite requests `user-details.read` for the user's name and email, plus `offline_access` so the session gets a refresh token. Cloudflare requires PKCE, which Appwrite handles for you. Cloudflare doesn't report whether the email is verified, so Appwrite stores it as unverified. If another user in your project already has that email, Appwrite redirects to your failure URL instead of signing the user in. To connect Cloudflare to that account, have the user sign in first and then start the OAuth flow from the browser, which links Cloudflare as a new [identity](/docs/products/auth/identities).

{% info title="Self-hosted availability" %}
Cloudflare sign-in is available on Appwrite Cloud and in self-hosted Appwrite 2.0 and later.
{% /info %}

# How to implement

To implement the Cloudflare OAuth adapter in Appwrite Auth, there are several steps you must complete:

## Step 1: Create an OAuth client on Cloudflare

You need the Super Administrator, Administrator, or OAuth Client Write role on the Cloudflare account. Log in to the Cloudflare dashboard, select your account, go to **Manage Account** > **OAuth clients**, and select **Create client**.

Fill in the configuration details:

- **Client name**: the name users see on Cloudflare's consent screen.
- **Response type** and **Grant type**: use the authorization code flow.
- **Token authentication method**: choose the method that sends the client secret in the request body (`client_secret_post`). Appwrite posts the client ID and secret with each token request.
- **Redirect URLs**: temporarily enter `https://temporary-endpoint.com/`. You'll replace it with the URI from Appwrite in the next step.

Select **Continue** and add the `user-details.read` scope. Select **Create client**, then save the **Client ID** and **Client Secret**. Cloudflare shows the secret only once.

New clients are private, so only members of the Cloudflare account that owns the client can authorize it. To let any Cloudflare user sign in, make the client public. Cloudflare requires a logo, a client URL, and domain verification of that URL first, and the change can't be undone.

## Step 2: Add Cloudflare OAuth adapter to your Appwrite project

For this step, you must [create an account on Appwrite Cloud](/sign-up) or [self-host Appwrite](/docs/advanced/self-hosting) if you haven't already. In your Appwrite project, head over to the **Auth** page, open the **Settings** tab, and click **Cloudflare** under the **OAuth2 Providers** section.

Paste the client's credentials into the **Client ID** and **Client Secret** fields, turn the provider on, and click **Update**. Appwrite checks the credentials with Cloudflare when you enable the provider and returns an error if Cloudflare rejects them.

Copy the **Redirect URI** shown in the form. On Appwrite Cloud it looks like `https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1/account/sessions/oauth2/callback/cloudflare/<PROJECT_ID>`, but always copy the exact value from the Console. Edit your OAuth client on Cloudflare and replace the temporary redirect URL with it.

## Step 3: Test the provider

Start a session from your app with the `OAuthProvider.Cloudflare` value. With the Web SDK:

```js
import { Client, Account, OAuthProvider } from 'appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>');

const account = new Account(client);

account.createOAuth2Session({
    provider: OAuthProvider.Cloudflare,
    success: 'https://example.com/success',
    failure: 'https://example.com/failed',
});
```

Add your app's domain as a web platform in your Appwrite project so Appwrite accepts the `success` and `failure` URLs. Follow the [OAuth 2 login](/docs/products/auth/oauth2#init) flow for other platforms and for server-side sign-in, and use [refresh tokens](/docs/products/auth/oauth2#refresh-tokens) to keep the Cloudflare access token current.

